{"kind":"FETCH","data":{"headers":{},"body":"{\"id\":7401470,\"site\":\"lawnsguide\",\"type\":\"lawn-care\",\"short_title\":\"lawn-care-tips-march\",\"language\":\"en\",\"published_time\":\"2026-07-23 00:20:53\",\"modified_time\":\"2026-07-23 00:20:53\",\"author\":\"James Miller\",\"img\":\"https://s.alicdn.com/@sc02/kf/A585c7ea3131d43e78243fd07ea9e7555W.jpg\",\"title\":\"Lawn Care Tips for March: Your Complete Early Spring Guide\",\"description\":\"Discover essential lawn care tips for March to kickstart a lush, healthy lawn. Learn about cleanup, mowing, fertilizing, weed control &amp; more.\",\"url\":\"/lawn-care/lawn-care-tips-march\",\"body\":\"<h1>Lawn Care Tips for March: Your Complete Early Spring Guide</h1>\\n\\n<p>March marks the beginning of the lawn care season for much of the country. As soil temperatures rise and daylight hours increase, your grass is waking from its winter dormancy and preparing for a season of vigorous growth. The work you put into your lawn during this transitional month sets the tone for the entire year ahead.</p>\\n\\n<p>Whether you maintain a <a href=\\\"/grass-types/bermuda-grass\\\">warm-season Bermuda grass</a> lawn in the South or a <a href=\\\"/grass-types/kentucky-bluegrass\\\">cool-season Kentucky bluegrass</a> lawn in the North, March presents unique opportunities and challenges. Timing your tasks correctly can mean the difference between a thick, vibrant lawn and one that struggles with weeds, bare patches, and disease throughout the growing season.</p>\\n\\n<p>This comprehensive guide walks you through every essential lawn care task for March, organized by priority and region, so you can build a strong foundation for a beautiful lawn all year long.</p>\\n\\n<h2>1. Spring Lawn Cleanup: Clearing the Way for Growth</h2>\\n\\n<p>Before you can focus on feeding, mowing, or treating your lawn, you need to remove the debris that accumulated over the winter months. A thorough spring cleanup is the single most important first step in your March lawn care routine.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Rake Away Dead Grass &amp; Debris</h3>\\n\\n<p>Winter leaves behind a layer of dead grass blades, fallen leaves, twigs, and other organic matter that can smother your lawn. This matted layer, sometimes called thatch buildup, blocks sunlight from reaching the soil surface and creates a damp environment that invites fungal diseases like snow mold.</p>\\n\\n<p>Use a sturdy <strong>leaf rake</strong> or a <strong>thatching rake</strong> to gently pull away dead material. For larger lawns, consider renting a <strong>power rake</strong> or <strong>dethatcher</strong> to make the job faster. Be thorough but gentle — you want to remove dead material without tearing out living grass crowns that are just beginning to green up.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Remove Winter Debris &amp; Inspect for Damage</h3>\\n\\n<p>Walk your entire lawn and pick up branches, rocks, and any trash that may have blown in during winter storms. Pay special attention to areas near roads and driveways where salt and sand may have accumulated. These areas often show signs of <a href=\\\"/lawn-care/salt-damage-repair\\\">salt damage</a> and may need extra attention.</p>\\n\\n<p>While cleaning, inspect your lawn for signs of:</p>\\n\\n<ul>\\n<li><strong>Snow mold</strong> — circular gray or pink patches of matted grass</li>\\n<li><strong>Vole damage</strong> — narrow tunnels or runways across the lawn surface</li>\\n<li><strong>Low spots</strong> — areas where water pooled over the winter</li>\\n<li><strong>Bare patches</strong> — spots where grass died back completely</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<p>Identifying these problems early allows you to address them before the growing season is fully underway. For detailed guidance on diagnosing common issues, check out our <a href=\\\"/lawn-care/lawn-disease-identification\\\">lawn disease identification guide</a>.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Tools You Will Need for Spring Cleanup</h3>\\n\\n<ul>\\n<li><strong>Leaf rake</strong> (flexible tines for gentle raking)</li>\\n<li><strong>Thatching rake</strong> (for moderate thatch removal)</li>\\n<li><strong>Lawn sweeper</strong> or <strong>power rake</strong> (for large properties)</li>\\n<li><strong>Garden gloves</strong> and <strong>knee pads</strong></li>\\n<li><strong>Wheelbarrow</strong> or <strong>yard waste bags</strong> for debris removal</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<h2>2. Mowing in March: Setting the Right Height &amp; Schedule</h2>\\n\\n<p>One of the most common questions homeowners ask is when to start mowing in the spring. The answer depends on your grass type and local climate, but there are universal principles that apply everywhere.</p>\\n\\n<h3>When to Make Your First Cut</h3>\\n\\n<p>Wait until your grass has actively started growing and has reached at least <strong>one-third higher than your target mowing height</strong>. For most cool-season grasses, this means waiting until blades are about 3.5 to 4 inches tall before cutting them back to 2.5 to 3 inches. For warm-season grasses emerging from dormancy, the first mowing typically happens when the grass reaches about 2 to 2.5 inches.</p>\\n\\n<p>Rushing to mow too early can stress grass that is still establishing its root system for the season. Patience during this transition period pays off with stronger, more resilient turf.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Sharpen Your Mower Blades</h3>\\n\\n<p>Before your first mow of the season, <strong>sharpen or replace your mower blades</strong>. Dull blades tear grass rather than cutting it cleanly, leaving ragged edges that turn brown and make your lawn more susceptible to disease. A sharp blade produces a clean cut that heals quickly and maintains a uniform, healthy appearance.</p>\\n\\n<p>Plan to sharpen your mower blades at least twice per season — once at the beginning of spring and again around midsummer. If you mow a large lawn or frequently encounter twigs and debris, you may need to sharpen them more often.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Mowing Heights by Grass Type</h3>\\n\\n<p>Setting the correct mowing height is critical for lawn health. Here is a quick reference for common grass types:</p>\\n\\n<ul>\\n<li><strong><a href=\\\"/grass-types/kentucky-bluegrass\\\">Kentucky Bluegrass</a></strong>: 2.5 to 3.5 inches</li>\\n<li><strong><a href=\\\"/grass-types/fine-fescue\\\">Fine Fescue</a></strong>: 2.5 to 4 inches</li>\\n<li><strong><a href=\\\"/grass-types/tall-fescue\\\">Tall Fescue</a></strong>: 3 to 4 inches</li>\\n<li><strong><a href=\\\"/grass-types/perennial-ryegrass\\\">Perennial Ryegrass</a></strong>: 2 to 3 inches</li>\\n<li><strong><a href=\\\"/grass-types/bermuda-grass\\\">Bermuda Grass</a></strong>: 1 to 2 inches</li>\\n<li><strong><a href=\\\"/grass-types/zoysia-grass\\\">Zoysia Grass</a></strong>: 1 to 2.5 inches</li>\\n<li><strong><a href=\\\"/grass-types/st-augustine-grass\\\">St. Augustine Grass</a></strong>: 2.5 to 4 inches</li>\\n<li><strong><a href=\\\"/grass-types/centipede-grass\\\">Centipede Grass</a></strong>: 1.5 to 2.5 inches</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<p>Never remove more than one-third of the grass blade in a single mowing. If your lawn has grown taller than expected during a warm spell, gradually reduce the height over two or three mowing sessions rather than scalping it all at once.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Leave the Clippings</h3>\\n\\n<p>Unless your lawn is plagued with disease or the clippings are excessively long and clumping, leave them on the lawn. Grass clippings decompose quickly and return valuable nitrogen and organic matter to the soil. This simple practice, often called <strong>grasscycling</strong>, can supply up to 25 percent of your lawn's annual nitrogen needs.</p>\\n\\n<h2>3. Fertilizing &amp; Soil Care: Feeding Your Lawn for the Season</h2>\\n\\n<p>March is an excellent time to evaluate your soil health and begin a fertilization program tailored to your grass type and regional conditions. Proper nutrition in early spring fuels strong root development and helps your lawn green up faster.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Test Your Soil Before Fertilizing</h3>\\n\\n<p>Before applying any fertilizer, conduct a <strong>soil test</strong> to determine your lawn's pH level and nutrient profile. Most grasses thrive in soil with a pH between 6.0 and 7.0. If your soil is too acidic (below 6.0), you may need to apply lime. If it is too alkaline (above 7.5), sulfur amendments can help bring it into range.</p>\\n\\n<p>Soil test kits are available at most garden centers, or you can send samples to your local cooperative extension service for a detailed analysis. Testing every two to three years is sufficient for most lawns. Learn more about understanding your results in our <a href=\\\"/lawn-care/soil-testing-guide\\\">complete soil testing guide</a>.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Choosing the Right Spring Fertilizer</h3>\\n\\n<p>The type of fertilizer you apply in March depends on your grass type:</p>\\n\\n<ul>\\n<li><strong>Cool-season grasses</strong> (Kentucky bluegrass, fescues, ryegrass): Apply a <strong>light application</strong> of balanced or nitrogen-rich fertilizer in early to mid-spring. A slow-release granular fertilizer with an N-P-K ratio such as 20-5-10 or 24-4-12 works well. Avoid heavy nitrogen applications, which can promote excessive top growth at the expense of root development and increase disease susceptibility.</li>\\n<li><strong>Warm-season grasses</strong> (Bermuda, Zoysia, St. Augustine, Centipede): Hold off on fertilizing until the grass has fully greened up and is actively growing, typically in late March to early April in warmer zones. Applying fertilizer too early can feed winter weeds and encourage tender growth that may be damaged by a late frost.</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<h3>Applying Lime or Sulfur</h3>\\n\\n<p>If your soil test indicates a pH imbalance, March is an ideal time to apply corrective amendments. Pelletized lime raises soil pH and is easy to spread with a standard broadcast spreader. Granular sulfur lowers pH for alkaline soils. Both amendments take several weeks to months to fully take effect, so applying them early in the season gives them time to work before peak growing conditions arrive.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Product Recommendations for Early Spring</h3>\\n\\n<ul>\\n<li><strong>Slow-release granular fertilizer</strong> (e.g., 20-5-10 for cool-season lawns)</li>\\n<li><strong>Pelletized lime</strong> (for acidic soils below pH 6.0)</li>\\n<li><strong>Soil sulfur</strong> (for alkaline soils above pH 7.5)</li>\\n<li><strong>Organic options</strong>: Milorganite, Espoma Organic Lawn Food, or compost top-dressing</li>\\n<li><strong>Drop or broadcast spreader</strong> for even application</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<p>Always follow the application rates listed on the product label. Over-fertilizing is one of the most common lawn care mistakes and can lead to thatch buildup, fertilizer burn, and increased pest activity.</p>\\n\\n<h2>4. Weed Control &amp; Pest Prevention in March</h2>\\n\\n<p>March is a critical window for weed control. Many common lawn weeds germinate in early spring, and addressing them before they become established saves significant time and effort later in the season.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Apply Pre-Emergent Herbicides</h3>\\n\\n<p>One of the most impactful things you can do in March is apply a <strong>pre-emergent herbicide</strong> to prevent crabgrass and other annual weeds from germinating. The key to pre-emergent success is timing — you must apply the product <strong>before soil temperatures consistently reach 55°F (13°C)</strong> at a depth of about two inches.</p>\\n\\n<p>A good natural indicator is the blooming of forsythia bushes. When forsythia blooms in your area, crabgrass germination is likely just a week or two away, making it the perfect time to apply your pre-emergent.</p>\\n\\n<p>Common pre-emergent active ingredients include:</p>\\n\\n<ul>\\n<li><strong>Prodiamine</strong> — long-lasting residual control (up to 6 months)</li>\\n<li><strong>Dithiopyr</strong> — offers both pre-emergent and early post-emergent control</li>\\n<li><strong>Pendimethalin</strong> — effective against a broad spectrum of annual grasses and broadleaf weeds</li>\\n<li><strong>Corn gluten meal</strong> — an organic alternative, though less potent than synthetic options</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<p>Important note: Do not apply pre-emergent herbicides if you plan to <a href=\\\"/lawn-care/overseeding-guide\\\">overseed your lawn</a> in the spring. These products prevent all grass seed from germinating, not just weed seeds. If overseeding is a priority, skip the pre-emergent and focus on post-emergent weed control instead.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Tackle Existing Weeds</h3>\\n\\n<p>For weeds that are already visible — such as dandelions, chickweed, henbit, and wild violet — spot-treat them with a <strong>selective post-emergent herbicide</strong> labeled for use on your grass type. Applying herbicide on a calm, dry day when temperatures are between 50°F and 80°F (10°C to 27°C) ensures the best results.</p>\\n\\n<p>For small infestations, hand-pulling is an effective and chemical-free option. Use a <strong>stand-up weeder tool</strong> or a <strong>dandelion digger</strong> to remove the entire root system and prevent regrowth.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Watch for Early-Season Pests</h3>\\n\\n<p>March is also a time to watch for signs of insect activity. Grubs that overwintered in the soil may begin feeding on grass roots as temperatures warm. If you notice irregular brown patches that lift easily from the soil (indicating root damage), you may have a grub problem.</p>\\n\\n<p>For grub control, consider applying a <strong>preventative grub control product</strong> containing chlorantraniliprole in early spring. This active ingredient is effective against grubs while being safer for beneficial insects and pollinators compared to older chemical options. For more information, visit our <a href=\\\"/lawn-care/grub-control-guide\\\">grub control guide</a>.</p>\\n\\n<h2>5. Overseeding, Aeration &amp; Repairing Winter Damage</h2>\\n\\n<p>If your lawn has thin areas, bare patches, or suffered significant winter damage, March can be a good time to repair and rejuvenate your turf — particularly for cool-season grass lawns.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Overseeding Cool-Season Lawns</h3>\\n\\n<p>While fall is the ideal time to overseed cool-season grasses, spring overseeding can be effective if done early enough. The goal is to establish new grass before the heat and drought stress of summer arrives.</p>\\n\\n<p>Follow these steps for successful spring overseeding:</p>\\n\\n<ol>\\n<li><strong>Mow low</strong>: Cut existing grass to about 1.5 to 2 inches to allow sunlight to reach the soil and new seedlings.</li>\\n<li><strong>Rake thoroughly</strong>: Remove thatch and debris to expose bare soil for seed contact.</li>\\n<li><strong>Core aerate</strong> (if possible): Aeration creates small holes in the soil that improve seed-to-soil contact and relieve compaction. Learn more in our <a href=\\\"/lawn-care/lawn-aeration-guide\\\">lawn aeration guide</a>.</li>\\n<li><strong>Spread seed</strong>: Use a broadcast spreader to apply grass seed at the recommended rate for your chosen variety. Select a seed blend that matches your existing lawn — see our <a href=\\\"/grass-types\\\">grass types guide</a> for help choosing the right seed.</li>\\n<li><strong>Top-dress lightly</strong>: Apply a thin layer (about 1/4 inch) of compost or topsoil over the seeded areas to protect the seed and retain moisture.</li>\\n<li><strong>Water consistently</strong>: Keep seeded areas moist by watering lightly two to three times per day until seedlings reach about 2 inches tall.</li>\\n</ol>\\n\\n<h3>Repairing Bare Patches</h3>\\n\\n<p>For small bare spots (less than a few feet across), you can use a <strong>patch repair product</strong> that combines grass seed, mulch, and fertilizer in one convenient mix. Simply loosen the soil in the bare area with a garden fork, spread the patch mix according to label directions, and keep the area consistently moist until new grass is established.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Should You Aerate in March?</h3>\\n\\n<p>Core aeration is beneficial for lawns with compacted soil or heavy thatch layers. For cool-season grasses, early spring aeration (March to early April) can be effective, though fall remains the preferred time. For warm-season grasses, wait until late spring or early summer when the grass is actively growing and can recover quickly from the aeration process.</p>\\n\\n<p>Signs your lawn may need aeration include:</p>\\n\\n<ul>\\n<li>Water pooling on the surface after rainfall</li>\\n<li>Difficulty pushing a screwdriver or probe into the soil</li>\\n<li>Heavy foot traffic areas that appear thin and compacted</li>\\n<li>Thatch layers thicker than half an inch</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<h3>Leveling Uneven Areas</h3>\\n\\n<p>Winter frost heave and settling can create bumps and low spots in your lawn. March is a good time to address these issues by top-dressing low areas with a mixture of sand and topsoil. Apply no more than half an inch at a time, allowing the grass to grow through the new layer before adding more. For significant grading issues, you may need to remove sod, regrade the soil, and replace the sod — a project best tackled when the grass is actively growing.</p>\\n\\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions About March Lawn Care</h2>\\n\\n<h3>Is it too early to fertilize my lawn in March?</h3>\\n\\n<p>For cool-season grasses such as Kentucky bluegrass, tall fescue, and perennial ryegrass, a light application of fertilizer in mid-to-late March is appropriate and beneficial. Use a slow-release formula and follow recommended application rates to avoid excessive top growth. For warm-season grasses like Bermuda, Zoysia, and St. Augustine, it is generally too early to fertilize in March unless you live in the warmest zones (USDA zones 9 and above) and your grass has fully broken dormancy. Fertilizing warm-season grass too early can promote weed growth and make tender new shoots vulnerable to late frosts.</p>\\n\\n<h3>When should I apply pre-emergent herbicide in March?</h3>\\n\\n<p>Apply pre-emergent herbicide when soil temperatures at a two-inch depth are consistently approaching 55°F (13°C). This typically falls somewhere in early to mid-March for the transition zone and southern regions, and late March to early April for northern areas. A helpful phenological indicator is the blooming of forsythia bushes — when you see those bright yellow flowers, it is time to apply your pre-emergent within the next week or two. Remember that pre-emergent herbicides will also prevent grass seed from germinating, so do not use them if you plan to overseed your lawn in the spring.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Should I rake my lawn in March or wait until it dries out?</h3>\\n\\n<p>It is best to wait until your lawn has dried out sufficiently before raking. Raking a soggy, saturated lawn can pull up healthy grass, damage crowns, and compact the soil further. A simple test is to walk on your lawn — if your footprints sink deeply and the soil feels spongy, wait another week or two. If the ground feels firm and the surface is mostly dry, gentle raking to remove dead material and debris is safe and beneficial. In northern climates where snow cover persists into March, wait until all snow has melted and the lawn has had several days to drain before beginning any raking or cleanup.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Can I overseed my lawn in March?</h3>\\n\\n<p>Yes, you can overseed cool-season lawns in March, and early spring overseeding can fill in thin areas and repair winter damage effectively. The key is to start early enough that new grass establishes before summer heat arrives. Prepare the soil by mowing low, raking, and ideally core aerating before spreading seed. Keep seeded areas consistently moist until seedlings are established. However, if you plan to use pre-emergent herbicide for crabgrass prevention, you will need to choose between overseeding and weed prevention, as the two practices are incompatible. For warm-season lawns, wait until late spring or early summer to overseed, when soil temperatures are consistently above 65°F (18°C).</p>\\n\\n<h3>How often should I water my lawn in March?</h3>\\n\\n<p>In most regions, natural rainfall in March is sufficient to meet your lawn's water needs. However, if you experience an unusually dry March with little precipitation, supplemental watering may be necessary. Apply about half an inch to one inch of water per week, either through rainfall or irrigation, to support early spring green-up. If you have newly overseeded areas, those spots will need more frequent, lighter watering — typically two to three brief watering sessions per day — to keep the seed and soil surface consistently moist. Avoid heavy watering that creates puddles or runoff, as this can wash away seed and promote fungal diseases. Always water in the early morning to minimize evaporation and reduce the risk of overnight moisture that encourages disease.</p>\\n\\n<h2>Regional Timing Tips for March Lawn Care</h2>\\n\\n<p>Because climate varies dramatically across the country, here is a quick regional breakdown to help you time your March lawn care tasks appropriately:</p>\\n\\n<h3>Northern Regions (USDA Zones 3–5)</h3>\\n\\n<p>Snow may still be on the ground in early March. Focus on late-March tasks such as initial cleanup, light raking once the lawn dries, and preparing your mower for the season. Soil temperatures are typically still too cold for fertilization or pre-emergent application. Be patient and let the lawn wake up naturally.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Transition Zone (USDA Zones 5–7)</h3>\\n\\n<p>This is where timing is most critical. Mid-to-late March is usually the sweet spot for cleanup, first mowing, light fertilization of cool-season lawns, and pre-emergent herbicide application. Monitor soil temperatures and watch for forsythia blooms as your natural cue.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Southern Regions (USDA Zones 7–10)</h3>\\n\\n<p>Warm-season lawns may already be breaking dormancy by early March. Begin cleanup and mowing as soon as growth starts. Apply pre-emergent herbicide early in the month. Hold off on fertilizing warm-season grasses until they have fully greened up, usually late March to early April. Cool-season lawns in the South (such as overseeded ryegrass) should be mowed regularly and can receive a light fertilizer application.</p>\\n\\n<h2>Final Thoughts</h2>\\n\\n<p>March lawn care is all about preparation and setting the stage for a healthy growing season. By cleaning up winter debris, sharpening your mower, testing your soil, applying the right fertilizer, preventing weeds, and repairing damage, you give your lawn the best possible start to the year.</p>\\n\\n<p>Remember that every lawn is unique. Your grass type, soil conditions, local climate, and the specific challenges your lawn faces will determine which tasks take priority. When in doubt, consult our <a href=\\\"/lawn-care\\\">complete lawn care guide</a> for season-by-season advice, or explore our <a href=\\\"/grass-types\\\">grass types library</a> to learn more about the specific needs of your turf.</p>\\n\\n<p>Start with the basics this March, stay consistent with your maintenance routine, and you will be rewarded with a thick, green, weed-free lawn that you can enjoy all season long.</p>\",\"tag\":null,\"is_online\":\"Y\",\"subtype\":null,\"slug\":null,\"cover_image\":null,\"status\":\"published\",\"created_at\":\"2026-07-23 00:31:15\"}","status":200,"url":""},"revalidate":3600,"tags":[]}